WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Speaker 1: We have a voicemail number. That&nbsp;
number is 2192. David P. I got a call. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.840
is pretty interesting. There's a caller who&nbsp;
isn't happy with an anti-death penalty argument&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.600
that I guess I made at some point. Take a&nbsp;
listen to this. This is very interesting.&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:20.720
Speaker 2: David. You did it again on&nbsp;
this on today's show, the death penalty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.960
arguing that we sometimes kill the wrong people&nbsp;
is not an argument against the death penalty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:33.600
dude. That's an argument against the judicial&nbsp;
judicial system. That is not an argument against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.520
the death penalty, just like I've heard you&nbsp;
say. It actually cost more to administer the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.600
death penalty than it does to keep somebody&nbsp;
alive. Okay. That's an argument against, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.320
government bureaucracy. That is not an argument&nbsp;
against the death penalty. Please state a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:54.720
argument against the death penalty, a moral one.
Speaker 1: Okay. So the claim that the caller is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:01.760
making is that me being against the death penalty&nbsp;
in part because we execute innocent people isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:07.760
a good argument against the morality of the death&nbsp;
penalty. It's an argument just against government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:13.360
being unable to decide for sure, to know for sure&nbsp;
whether someone is actually guilty. I actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.680
disagree with this caller. I think all of these&nbsp;
are valid arguments against the death penalty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:24.480
Some want to restrict arguments against the&nbsp;
death penalty only to why it's wrong to have it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:32.000
assuming we could execute it perfectly. But&nbsp;
the reality is we live in the real world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.760
and so we have to be able to&nbsp;
consider these other arguments. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:41.360
the main argument for me being against the death&nbsp;
penalty is that I believe it is wrong for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.960
government to put people to death. I believe it&nbsp;
is wrong for the government or the state to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:53.920
well, you killed someone, so now we kill you. I&nbsp;
think it's beneath us. I think it's retrograde.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:59.840
I think it's antiquated. I think it's cruel and&nbsp;
unusual. So that's the main reason I'm against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.680
the death penalty. It shouldn't be the business&nbsp;
of the government to be putting people to death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:11.600
In addition to that, we also have to&nbsp;
consider the justice system isn't perfect and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:19.040
we never know. In all cases, we I can't imagine&nbsp;
we would ever be at 100% only guilty people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.800
get convicted. And so that's another reason&nbsp;
because it is irreversible, the death penalty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.640
is irreversible. And we want to maintain because&nbsp;
any justice system sometimes makes makes mistakes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.440
I would rather be able to release someone from&nbsp;
a life sentence. If they didn't really do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:40.560
then kill them. In addition, there's the fiscal&nbsp;
conservatism argument where it costs more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:46.080
to put someone on death row and ultimately carry&nbsp;
out the execution. So if you care about that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:54.800
that's also an argument we all. So all of these&nbsp;
things are valid arguments by itself. I agree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.520
Saying the death penalty should be abolished&nbsp;
because sometimes we kill innocent people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:06.720
I'm with you. By itself, that's not a strong&nbsp;
enough argument. But it's fine to include it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.880
part of a bigger picture about the death penalty&nbsp;
and its problems. And there's other issues as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.960
well, which I brought up in the past about the&nbsp;
death penalty. So I respectfully disagree with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:21.120
the caller that it's not a valid component of&nbsp;
opposition to the death penalty, but by itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.440
it is not nearly strong enough. Let me know your&nbsp;
thoughts. We have a great bonus show for you&nbsp;&nbsp;

